-modify-write on a shared dict, so concurrent increments overwrote each other. `snapshot()` copied that dict while other threads were inserting new keys, which could produce a copy taken mid-update.\n\n**Fix:** Each counter name now maps to a small `_Cell` object with its own lock.\n- `incr` takes only that counter's lock, so different counters don't contend.\n- A short registry lock guards the name-to-cell mapping (lookups, inserts, removals).\n- `snapshot()` holds the registry lock while building a plain `dict` of name to value, so the copy never sees a mid-insert dict.\n- `get`, `reset`, and the public API are unchanged.\n\n**Trade-offs:**\n- `snapshot()` is consistent per counter, not across counters. Two counters read at slightly different moments can be inconsistent with each other. That matches the old semantics; it was never a transaction.\n- If `reset(name)` runs while an `incr` on that name is in flight, the in-flight increment can be discarded. It is equivalent to the increment having happened just before the reset.\n- I kept the `time.sleep(0)` inside the per-counter lock, so the test still exercises the read-to-write window.\n\nI did not commit anything.", "ttft_ms": 1460, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 28785, "uuid": "0df09a14-6750-4673-8699-dca4a6570e34", "ttft_stream_ms": 830, "time_to_request_ms": 108, "first_content_frame_ms": 831, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T17:06:19.950Z"}